  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the 20 files needed to plan the listener-container threading refactor across the three repos, ordered most important first:\n\n**spring-kafka (primary implementation, 11 files)**\n- `ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer.java` / `KafkaMessageListenerContainer.java` \u2014 the two container types at the center of the refactor (single-threaded \u2192 concurrent wrapper spawning N child containers)\n- `AbstractMessageListenerContainer.java`, `ContainerProperties.java`, `GenericMessageListenerContainer.java`, `MessageListenerContainer.java` \u2014 the shared container contract/lifecycle and runtime properties (including `listenerTaskExecutor`) that both container types extend\n- `ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory.java`, `AbstractKafkaListenerContainerFactory.java`, `KafkaListenerContainerFactory.java` \u2014 factories that construct containers from `@KafkaListener` endpoints and propagate `concurrency`\n- `KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry.java`, `KafkaListener.java`, `KafkaListenerAnnotationBeanPostProcessor.java`, `EnableKafka.java`, `KafkaBootstrapConfiguration.java` \u2014 annotation-driven wiring where per-listener `concurrency` is read and containers are registered\n\n**spring-boot (autoconfiguration/properties consumer, 3 files)**\n- `KafkaProperties.java` \u2014 exposes `spring.kafka.listener.concurrency` externally\n- `KafkaAutoConfiguration.java`, `KafkaAnnotationDrivenConfiguration.java` \u2014 wire the factory bean and apply properties to it\n\n**spring-framework (core abstractions, 3 files)**\n- `TaskExecutor.java`, `AsyncTaskExecutor.java`, `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor.java` \u2014 the threading abstraction each concurrent child container uses to run its own consumer thread (`ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer` defaults to `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` per child)\n\nThis confirms the dependency chain in the task: spring-framework's `TaskExecutor`/`SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` underpins spring-kafka's per-child threading in `ConcurrentMessageListenerContainer`, which spring-kafka's factory/annotation layer exposes as `concurrency`, which spring-boot's `KafkaProperties`/`KafkaAutoConfiguration` then surfaces as `spring.kafka.listener.concurrency`.",
